# archlint's on-disk cache. Regenerated by every archlint run, grows without
# bound (git-history.redb had reached 1.88 MB), and was being packed into the
# published tarball because it lives under src/npm-package/. Never tracked.
.archlint-cache/

# npm-package gate modules: generated by sync-package-content.js at prepack
# (copy of src/gates, shipped via package.json files[]). Never tracked — tracked
# byte-identical copies of the same test files trip archlint CodeClone + Gate 5a.
src/npm-package/gates/

# npm-package adapters: generated by sync-package-content.js at prepack from
# githooks/adapters/ (199 files, byte-identical pure copies). Never tracked.
#
# P1-E step 1 of the mirror-duplication decision (docs/plans/2026-11-08-mirror-
# duplication-decision.md): the largest mirror tree moves out of version control.
# npm's `files` field cannot point outside the package, so the published tarball
# still contains a copy -- what changes is that the SOURCE repo holds one.
#
# Editing a file here is now impossible to get wrong rather than merely
# detectable: the tree no longer exists until prepack generates it, so there is no
# second copy to edit by mistake (#683 shipped 4953 bytes of stale gate-3.sh this
# way, and v0.27.3 shipped a mirror-only fix that the next publish rolled back).
#
# mirror-parity's CI semantics change accordingly: from "are the two committed
# trees equal" to "does the generator reproduce the canonical tree byte-for-byte",
# which tests the generator instead of trusting that two copies happened to match.
src/npm-package/adapters/

# npm-package hooks/lib, build-integrity and skills: P1-E step 2, generated by
# src/npm-package/scripts/generate-mirrors.cjs from githooks/lib/ (21 files),
# src/build-integrity/ (9) and skills/ (67). Same argument as the adapters block
# above: 97 byte-identical copies stop being editable by mistake.
#
# The correctness claim is the generator's, not git's -- these trees are checked by
# scripts/__tests__/generated-mirror-reproducibility.test.cjs (REQ-E1-05/06), which
# regenerates from a clean canonical tree and asserts byte equality plus the file
# count. A tree that drifts back into version control fails REQ-E1-06.
#
# src/principles/ joins this group in P1-E step 4. It was held back because it was
# being edited in place by another workstream, and two producers writing one tree
# means whichever ran last decides the content. That work has landed, so the same
# argument applies as for the trees above.
src/npm-package/hooks/lib/
src/npm-package/build-integrity/
src/npm-package/skills/
# P1-E step 3: these two shipped in the package with no generator and no mirror
# pair, so nothing ever compared them against their source. They are generated now
# (GENERATED_TREES), so the tarball copy is produced at prepack like the rest.
src/npm-package/mutation/
src/npm-package/mock-policy/
src/npm-package/principles/

# src/npm-package/hooks/ and src/npm-package/gates/ stay TRACKED on purpose.
#
# Not an oversight, and not "we ran out of time". `hooks/` is a curated file list
# (57 entries in HOOK_MIRROR_FILES, scripts/check-hook-mirror.sh:54), not a tree
# copy: `githooks/` also holds `__tests__/`, the adapters subtree, `verify.sh` and
# `AGENTS.md`, none of which ship. Giving it a generator would mean a second place
# that enumerates the same 57 files, and two enumerations of one set is the exact
# failure P1-E exists to remove -- the pair table, the generator and the manifest
# had already drifted from each other three ways.
#
# It already has the strongest guarantee in the repo: a per-file `cmp` against
# canonical for every entry, which fails closed on a missing or differing file.
# Untracking it would move it from "checked byte-for-byte" to "produced by a
# generator that has to be kept in sync with a shell array". That is a downgrade.
#
# `gates/` is a stale `files[]` entry for a directory that does not exist; removing
# it is a packaging change, tracked separately.

